//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="one two three\nat-end\nempty: at-end")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: std.net, err.row, err.else
//!
//! A stream is bytes, not messages: three writes arrive as one sequence and
//! a reader cuts it wherever it asks to. This file writes `"one "`,
//! `"two "` and `"three"` from one end, closes it, and reads 4, 4 and 5
//! bytes from the other — proving both halves of the contract at once.
//!
//! **`write` is complete, which is why there is no `write_all`.** The
//! builtin loops internally, so each of the three calls delivers its whole
//! string or raises; nothing here retries, and a `write_all` in `std.net`
//! would be ceremony around a hazard this surface does not have.
//!
//! **The end of the stream is a ROW, not a length.** The fourth read misses,
//! because the peer closed — handled with a plain `else` value, exactly as
//! `tests/fs/fd_stream.lu` handles a file's `eof`. What this test does NOT
//! do is claim to know WHICH tag the miss carried: a handler cannot tell
//! them apart on the executing lane (F-0052 — a `match` in a handler binds
//! its first arm, silently), so "the miss ends the stream" is all a caller
//! may honestly conclude, and it is why `read_all` is a reviewed contract in
//! the module header instead of a function.
//!
//! Determinism, restated at sc40 because v0.2.8 falsified the reason this
//! header used to give. It said: the client writes and CLOSES before the
//! server accepts, so all thirteen bytes are already buffered by the host
//! when the reads run and "a read of `n` returns exactly `n` while bytes
//! remain". **That last clause was never a property of `read` and it is
//! not one now** — `read` is one read of UP TO `n` bytes, and until
//! `[os.net.nodelay]` shipped, Nagle coalesced these three small writes so
//! reliably that the difference never showed. It shows now: with
//! `TCP_NODELAY` on by default the three writes are three segments, and a
//! read that spans a segment boundary can come back short (F-0112, which
//! is exactly how `net.write`'s doc example failed).
//!
//! **What makes THIS file deterministic is alignment, and that is worth
//! saying out loud**: each read asks for exactly one write's length — 4,
//! 4, 5 against `"one "`, `"two "`, `"three"` — so no read ever spans a
//! boundary, whether the host delivers the segments separately or
//! coalesces them. A read of 13, or of 7, would be the flaky shape. A
//! reader that needs a count it did not write asks `read_all` or loops.
//!
//! Byte counts are ASCII on purpose — a byte-count read of non-ASCII text
//! can split a code point and raise `utf8` (documented on `net.read`,
//! filed as F-0035/F-0044).

use std.net

fn main() -> !int {
    var srv = net.listen(net.loopback(0))?
    let p = net.port(srv)?
    var cli = net.connect(net.loopback(p))?
    net.write(mut cli, "one ")?
    net.write(mut cli, "two ")?
    net.write(mut cli, "three")?
    net.close(take cli)?
    var conn = net.accept(mut srv)?
    let a = net.read(mut conn, 4)?
    let b = net.read(mut conn, 4)?
    let c = net.read(mut conn, 5)?
    print("{a}{b}{c}")
    let ended = net.read(mut conn, 4) else "at-end"
    print("{ended}")
    net.close(take conn)?
    // A peer that says nothing and goes away: the first read is already the
    // end of the stream.
    var quiet = net.connect(net.loopback(p))?
    net.close(take quiet)?
    var qconn = net.accept(mut srv)?
    let nothing = net.read(mut qconn, 16) else "at-end"
    print("empty: {nothing}")
    net.close(take qconn)?
    net.close_listener(take srv)?
    0
}
